WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.969 --> 00:00:04.770
The outcome of a coin flip is completely random.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.899
Only it’s not random at all.

00:00:06.899 --> 00:00:11.320
If I’d known exactly how the coin started
and what forces acted on it, and if I could

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.250
do the calculations quick enough I could have
predicted the outcome.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:20.090
It only looked random because there were these
hidden variables I didn’t take into account.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:25.250
We’ll call this classical randomness because
this is the only type of randomness allowed

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:30.980
in classical physics, things only look random
because we don’t know all the factors influencing

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:31.980
them.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.900
The situation in quantum mechanics, though,
is quite different.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.460
Remember we met the wavefunction last time
and we said it contains each of the possible

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.020
things the particle could be doing.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:48.510
As we know, when someone or something looks,
it has to just be doing one of these things.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:53.360
For example, in the double slit experiment
if we put detectors behind each door, we’d

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.090
see each particle going through just one slit.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.880
But which one?

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.010
Standard quantum mechanics says that this
is truly random.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:08.370
Unlike in classical physics, there is no extra
information you could get or extra calculations

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.740
you can do that could let you predict which
it will be.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.850
It’s not that the particle really was doing
one thing all along- if you hadn’t measured

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.130
it, it would still be a superposition.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:25.080
But when you do look, it has to choose only
one option, and because that choice is truly

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.090
random, there is no cause for it.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:31.600
There is no reason why this particle is measured
at door 1 and not 2.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:32.600
But!

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.310
That doesn’t mean that every option is equally
likely.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:37.670
Consider this example.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:42.880
We do the double slit experiment again, but
this time we put one of the doors a bit farer

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:43.880
away.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:48.200
We still don’t know which door the particle
went through so it must still be in a superposition

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.920
of going through both... but if we measure
it, it should be more likely to go through

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.420
the closer door right?

00:01:55.420 --> 00:02:00.450
This fact is reflected in the wavefunction
through those coefficients that I’ve been

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:01.450
ignoring.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:04.170
In this example they could be these numbers.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:09.060
You interpret this as the bigger the number,
the more likely the corresponding state is

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.619
to happen.

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:14.830
In this example, the probability that the
particle is measured at the closer door is

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:17.780
3/4, and the farer door is 1/4.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:23.090
The exact mathematical rule for this is very
simple, but is a bit of a tangent at the moment,

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:26.980
so I’ll put that in a short video by itself.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:31.680
Quantum mechanics does have an element of
true unpredictability, but that doesn’t

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.750
mean that the theory is useless.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:38.099
You can’t predict where one particular particle
is going to go from its wavefunction, but

00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:42.281
if there are a bunch of particles with that
wavefunction, quantum mechanics let’s you

00:02:42.281 --> 00:02:45.770
predict their overall behaviour very accurately.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:51.340
There’s one other aspect of the measurement
rule: that measurement causes the wavefunction

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:52.900
to collapse.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.480
Say something measures a particle and finds
it in a particular state.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.650
What does the wavefunction look like now?

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:04.130
Remember the rule is that the wavefunction
is in a superposition of all possible states.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:09.530
But now, only this state is possible, so the
function collapses to just this.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:13.000
This actually has a dramatic effect.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:18.090
In the double slit experiment, if I am measuring
which slit each particle goes through that

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:23.440
collapses their wavefunction so that now,
each particle really is just going through

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:24.750
one door.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:28.830
That means they’re not interfering with
themselves, and so we get the regular pattern,

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.630
the particles land in two bunches.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:34.340
So here’s an interesting scenario.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.860
Say Bob is measuring which door each particle
goes through.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:44.000
Bob sees this particle go through slit 1,
so he says the wavefunction collapses to this.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.140
But what if Bob didn’t tell Alice his result?

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:50.610
Is the wavefunction collapsed for her or not?

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.430
Is there an experiment she could do to tell?

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:59.930
Feel free to pause the video if you want to
think about it.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:04.890
The answer is, if one person or thing does
the measurement, it doesn’t matter if no

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:09.280
one else knows the result, the wavefunction
is collapsed.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.269
This is a bit surprising because imagine if
Alice goes and measures which slit the particle

00:04:14.269 --> 00:04:16.940
goes through straight after Bob.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:22.270
Her result looks completely random to her,
just as if the particle was in a superposition.

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:25.430
But, this isn’t true randomness anymore.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:30.970
She’ll get the same result as Bob, but since
she doesn’t know his result, it looks random

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:33.990
just because she is missing that information.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:37.710
Instead what she should do is look at the
pattern on the screen.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:43.020
As we said just before, because the wavefunction
is collapsed, she’ll see the particles land

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.860
in two bunches.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:48.210
This explains one of the big questions about
quantum mechanics.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:50.590
Why don’t we see quantum weirdness all the
time?

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:54.830
Why don’t I see an interference pattern
when I throw apples and don’t watch which

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:56.220
door they go through?

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:00.470
Because, even if I’m not watching, plenty
of other things are.

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:04.840
The apple will interact with air and light
and make sounds etc.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:07.690
They collapse the wavefunction for me.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:12.210
So Quantum superpositions are rare because
they are so fragile.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:16.810
We talk about quantum effects with very small
particles in very controlled environments

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:24.120
because those particles really aren’t interacting
with much, so can stay in a superposition.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.430
And now you guys now know the two aspects
of the measurement rule:

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:32.900
first, The outcome is random, but the probabilities
come from the coefficients in the wavefunction.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:37.080
And second after a measurement, the wavefunction
collapses.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.580
And so it’s question time!

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:44.080
First, what if there are three slits and you
only have a detector at one.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:49.720
What does the wavefunction of a particle that
goes through look like before and after?

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:52.650
The second question is about what counts as
a measurement.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:57.020
I kind of implied that interactions with air
and light count.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:00.100
Do you think all interactions are measurements?

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:04.280
What about if a machine does a measurement
and then, without storing it in memory, prints

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.370
the result, and burns it.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:09.000
Is the wavefunction still collapsed?

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.980
And finally one about interpretations.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:14.000
What do you think of quantum randomness?

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.289
Do you understand why physicists had problems
with it?

00:06:18.289 --> 00:06:22.280
As you may know, there are hidden variable
alternatives to Quantum mechanics that don’t

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:25.750
have true randomness; does this make them
more appealing?

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:29.840
Is there anything you don’t like about hidden
variable theories?

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:34.620
Finally thank you so much for all for your
answers to the slightly more philosophical

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:37.700
questions last time, and sorry I fell behind
answering.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:38.700
But it was so fun to see everyone have such
different, but well considered opinions.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.729
I’m really looking forward your answers
this time.

